Description: Draws samples from a posterior distribution and estimates the
    marginal likelihood (model evidence) with incremental mixture
    importance sampling (IMIS). Starting from a sample of the prior, the
    importance sampling function is built up iteratively as a defensive
    mixture of the prior and multivariate normal components centered where
    the current importance weights are largest, optionally after an
    optimization stage that locates local posterior modes ("IMIS-opt").
    Designed for calibrating mechanistic and simulation models, whose
    posteriors are often ridge-shaped or multimodal. Computations are
    done on the log scale; several components can be added per iteration;
    new draws can come from the newest components (Raftery and Bao, 2010
    <doi:10.1111/j.1541-0420.2010.01399.x>) or from the whole current
    mixture (Steele, Raftery and Emond, 2006
    <doi:10.1198/106186006X132358>); and likelihood evaluations can run
    in parallel through the 'future' framework.
